<h2>Section 1: Multiple Choice (40 points)</h2>
<p><strong>1.1.</strong> What is the primary function of the cerebral cortex?</p>
<ul>
    <li>a) To store long-term memories</li>
    <li>b) To process sensory information and control voluntary movements</li>
    <li>c) To regulate basic life functions</li>
    <li>d) To produce hormones</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>1.2.</strong> Which neurotransmitter is primarily responsible for the “fight or flight” response?</p>
<ul>
    <li>a) Dopamine</li>
    <li>b) Serotonin</li>
    <li>c) Norepinephrine</li>
    <li>d) Acetylcholine</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>1.3.</strong> The process by which the body maintains a stable internal environment is called:</p>
<ul>
    <li>a) Homeostasis</li>
    <li>b) Neuroplasticity</li>
    <li>c) Synaptic pruning</li>
    <li>d) Habituation</li>
</ul>

<h2>Section 2: True/False (20 points)</h2>
<p><strong>2.1.</strong> [T/F] The amygdala is involved in the processing of fear and emotional memory.</p>
<p><strong>2.2.</strong> [T/F] Humans have 46 chromosomes.</p>
<p><strong>2.3.</strong> [T/F] Classical conditioning was discovered by Ivan Pavlov.</p>

<h2>Section 3: Short Answer (30 points)</h2>
<p><strong>3.1.</strong> Define classical conditioning and provide one example from everyday life.</p>

<p><strong>3.2.</strong> Explain the difference between a neuron’s resting potential and its action potential. Use a simple diagram to illustrate the key stages.</p>

<p><strong>3.3.</strong> What is the role of the hippocampus in memory formation?</p>

<h2>Section 4: Essay (30 points)</h2>
<p><strong>4.1.</strong> Describe the stages of the memory process (encoding, storage, and retrieval). Which stage is most susceptible to forgetting and why?</p>

